course: dictionary
lesson: 11144
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many common words have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists them as numbered meanings, and the context tells you which one the writer means. «A lazy beginning that suddenly turns into a wild, dreamy adventure» uses wild to mean exciting and not controlled, not living in nature.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «Food changes how wild animals behave» is about animals living in nature, not looked after by people. Wild animals are the opposite of pets and farm animals. Ask: which meaning fits here, and does the sentence still make sense when I put it in?
explain: Wild can also be a noun or an adverb. The wild means nature, away from people. Phrases change the meaning too: a wild guess is a guess made without thinking, and run wild means to grow or behave without control. Read the whole group of words, not just one.
fact: Wild can mean living or growing in nature, not looked after by people: wild animals, wild flowers.
fact: Wild can describe land that is natural, with few people or buildings.
fact: Wild can mean stormy and strong, as in weather, or not controlled and very noisy, as in a wild party.
fact: A wild guess is a guess made without thinking carefully.
fact: Never feed or touch wild animals, because they can bite and carry illness. Do not eat wild berries or mushrooms unless an adult who knows them says they are safe.
exercise: «Food changes how wild animals behave.» What does wild mean here? | *Living in nature, not looked after by people | Stormy and strong | Made up without thinking | Very angry
exercise: Kiran says, «That was a wild guess!» What does wild guess mean? | A guess made by a farmer | *A guess made without thinking carefully | A guess about wild animals | A guess that is very accurate
exercise: «Raccoons were brought to Europe and some escaped; now they live wild in Germany.» Find and copy the word that tells you the raccoons live in nature. | escaped | Europe | Germany | *wild
test: Why must you never feed or touch wild animals? | They are always tame and friendly | They are safer when people feed them | They like being given food from people | *They can bite and carry illness
test: Which phrase means to grow or behave without control? | in the wild | *run wild | wildlife | wild animal
exam: Which meaning of wild fits «a wild party»? | *Not controlled and very noisy | Stormy and strong | Far away from people's homes | Living in nature
